      ":question/math-academy-id": "e-458",
      ":question/problem": "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int x^3\\sqrt{x^2 + 1}\\, \\textrm{d}x.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u = x^2+1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral in terms of $u,$ and then express our final answer in terms of $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2}\\cdot x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\overset{u-1}{\\overset{⏞}{{x}^{2}}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\overset{\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u}{\\overset{⏞}{x\\,\\text{d}x}} \\\\  & =\\int (u-1)\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int (u-1){u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int ({u}^{3/2}-{u}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}({x}^{2}+1)^{5/2}-\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}+1)^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{5}}-\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
