        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-113751",
        ":question/problem": "Given that $u=x+1,$ which integral is equivalent to $\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x+1}}\\,\\text{d}x?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Since $u=x+1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe also note that \n$x=u-1.$\n\nNow we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x+1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{(u-1)^{2}}{\\sqrt{u}}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int ({u}^{2}-2u+1){u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int ({u}^{3/2}-2{u}^{1/2}+{u}^{-1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int (\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}-2\\sqrt{u}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}})\\,\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
