        ":question/id": "056e1c78-a9bb-5b9a-a3a2-7b12402a4162",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-2615",
        ":question/problem": "Calculate the integral $\\int {x}^{2}\\sqrt{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=1-x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that\n$x=1-u.$\n\nNow we can solve the integral in terms of $u,$ and then express our final answer in terms of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\sqrt{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int (1-u)^{2}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\int ({u}^{2}-2u+1)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\int ({u}^{5/2}-2{u}^{3/2}+{u}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-(\\frac{2}{7}{u}^{7/2}-\\frac{4}{5}{u}^{5/2}+\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{7}\\sqrt{(1-x)^{7}}+\\frac{4}{5}\\sqrt{(1-x)^{5}}-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{(1-x)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
