        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-49387",
        ":question/problem": "Given that $u=1+{x}^{2}$, which integral is equivalent to $\\int \\frac{{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Since $u=1+{x}^{2}$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,x\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that\n${x}^{2}=u-1$\n\nNow we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}} & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}\\cdot {x}^{2}\\cdot x\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (u-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int ({u}^{1/2}-{u}^{-1/2})\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
